# Tokens libFuzzer should splice in rather than discover byte by byte.
# The grammar is RFC 9110's Accept-Encoding: a comma separated list of
# codings, each optionally carrying a ";q=" weight.
"zstd"
"gzip"
"deflate"
"br"
"identity"
"compress"
"*"
","
";"
"q"
"Q"
"="
"q=0"
"q=0.0"
"q=0.000"
"q=1"
"q=1.0"
";q=0"
" "
"\x09"
# One case variant, one superstring and one prefixed form per token
# the target searches for. The superstrings are what the boundary
# check exists to reject, so they are worth splicing rather than
# leaving the fuzzer to assemble a letter at a time.
#
# "brotli" is the only one of these that people actually write - the
# zstd and gzip forms below are invented for the same purpose. It is
# also the hardest case, "br" being two characters and a prefix of it.
"ZSTD"
"zstdlib"
"x-zstd"
"BR"
"brotli"
"x-br"
"GZIP"
"gzipped"
"x-gzip"
